/* Only Textual design tokens, no colour literals.

   That is what makes every built-in theme work for free. The four semantic colours
   that ARE literals live in widgets.py (GOOD/BAD/WARN/FAINT) and are deliberately
   not themed: bare `red` resolves to #ff0000 and bare `green` to #008000, harsh and
   nearly unreadable respectively on a dark warm-earth background. */

Screen { background: $surface; }

#bottom { dock: bottom; height: auto; }

/* The theme picker sits above the footer, NOT in a modal: a ModalScreen would cover the
   interface being previewed, which is the whole point of previewing. */
#theme-picker {
    height: 3;
    border: round $accent;
    border-title-align: left;
    border-subtitle-align: left;
    padding: 0 1;
}

#prompt {
    height: 3;
    border: round $accent;
    border-title-align: left;
    border-subtitle-align: left;
}
#prompt.error { border: round $error; }

/* FOUR ROWS, NOT TWO: a status row plus one row per key group (write, view, nav).
   The author:
   "you can learn from daylogs to stack key options instead of laying them flat."
   FIXED rather than auto, and daylogs' reason is the one that matters -- a footer that
   collapses when a scope's groups happen to fit makes its height depend on which tab you
   are on, so every tab switch reflows the table above it. A predictable frame is worth
   the row. A scope with no `view` keys simply leaves that line blank. */
#keyfooter {
    height: 4;
    padding: 0 1;
    color: $text-muted;
}

/* AIR ABOVE THE PORTFOLIO. It follows the sleeve block directly, and without this its
   title reads as another line of that block rather than the start of a section. */
#port-head { margin-top: 1; }

/* AIR BETWEEN SECTIONS. The author: "you should have some breadthing air between sections. now
   everything is scrambled together."

   They are right, and every gap was missing for the same reason: nothing here had a vertical
   margin, so a pane title sat directly on its first figure, the capital ledger sat directly
   on the last sleeve line, and the bets charts sat directly on the note. Blank lines inside
   a Static would fix some of it, but not the boundaries BETWEEN widgets -- and those are
   exactly the section boundaries. So the gaps live here, one rule per boundary, rather than
   as padding smuggled into the text. */
.pane-title {
    color: $text;
    text-style: bold;
    padding: 0 1;
    margin-bottom: 1;
    text-wrap: nowrap;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
}

.panel-body {
    padding: 0 1;
    height: auto;
}

/* The capital ledger is a section of the dashboard, not a continuation of the sleeves, so it
   gets air above its heading as well as below.

   BOTH SIDES ARE SPELLED OUT because an id selector out-specifies `.pane-title`, and setting
   only `margin-top` here silently dropped the `margin-bottom: 1` that rule provides -- the
   heading sat straight on its own column row while every other title had its gap. */
#cap-head { margin-top: 1; margin-bottom: 1; }

/* The note is about the row above it and the charts are about the whole table, so each
   needs to be visibly separate from what precedes it. */
#bet-note, #bet-charts, #bet-done { margin-top: 1; }

.muted { color: $text-muted; padding: 0 1; }

/* overflow-x hidden so left/right keep switching tabs rather than scrolling the
   table sideways. */
DataTable {
    height: 1fr;
    min-height: 5;
    margin: 0 1;
    overflow-x: hidden;
}

/* A table that SHARES a scroll container with something below it sizes to its own rows
   instead of claiming the space. `1fr` there would push the charts and the ledger off the
   bottom of a container that is already scrollable, which is the one place height:1fr is
   actively wrong. */
#bet-table, #cap-table { height: auto; }

#help-body {
    padding: 1 2;
    background: $panel;
    border: round $accent;
    width: 70;
    max-width: 90%;
    height: auto;
    max-height: 90%;
}

HelpScreen { align: center middle; }
